\begin_layout Standard
- Thurow - generating inequality - https://archive.org/details/generatinginequa0000unse - however thurow seems more concerned with distribution (upper middle class gaining at the cost of lower and higher classes - he says that just based on aggregate descriptive statistics).
 He highlights that distribution of earnings is different from distribution of wealth (this is also relevant to my theory).
 He also talks about how physical wealth and human wealth cannot be added (in Weberian framework - opportunity or Chancen permit just that).
 What's relevant is the fairness of distribution game - where the maximisation of risk-neutral values seems to be the accepted goal from Rawls.
 He does consider the idea of a lottery of distribution and that is essential to my theory.
 He talks about labour queue - by emphasising the relative position in the queue - rather than a market of skills.
 He says workers compete but not based on willingness to accept low wages but for position based on background characteristics.
 He explains this further how daily bidding would disincentivise education-sharing among professors - because of there being less incentive is the professors were competing against students.
 This also explains - Thurow points out - how employers justify the value of their training by not letting trained labour compete with their previous selves - the untrained labour.
 Thurow uses this to explain how discrimination against blacks prevails - as he rejects the explanation that white employees must have skills that they don't pass on to black employees (i.e.
 the ideas that hostility with blacks getting promoted imposes some costs).
 Instead he argues that monopolisation - the promoter of social distance - causes such such discrimination to prevail.
 Thurow has his some ideas about interdependent preferences/utility - which is relates with psychologists' ideas of envy.
 In my model,
 utility is wealth based and relative preferences don't matter - except through reference dependence.
 He discusses wealth-inheritance in great details - but an important detail that I must mention is that my model simplifies wealth accumulation as a social class transition (page 156).
\end_layout

