\begin_layout Standard
Inequality - is relevant to education - we only seem to argue that increasing education access would improve social conditions - but given that a lot of education is signaling,
 incrasing signaling itself may not increase welfare as much (cite:
 Aryal Buller Lange - https://www.jstor.org/stable/27155932?seq=1) .
 The presented paper attempts to link the effects of inequality and effort to understand the policy implications for education.The results show that high participation costs can balance a fall in income differences whereas higher income differences can prevail if participation costs in the competitions affected by social capital investments.
 The results also highlight that a reference-dependent utilty can serve to make a segregation of status competitions.
 These results seem aligned with the conventional wisdow in how equality of opportunity may come in conflict with equality of outcome (see Heckman,
 Mosso,
 
